gitrl-z (0.7.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Rewind every local branch from a dial over the graph. The reflogs of all
    local branches are folded into the list of distinct moments the repository
    has passed through, two entries written in the same second counting as one
    moment. All branches in the sidebar shows those reflogs merged and ordered
    by time, each row carrying the branch it belongs to, and the dial steps
    through the moments and puts every branch back where it stood at the one
    it rests on. The moment can also be typed to the second, in which case the
    dial resolves it to the state in force then.
  * Plan a branch that did not exist at the chosen moment for removal. The
    reset plan gains a set of deletions alongside its targets, and the
    generated command gains a branch -D line. The checked out branch is never
    offered for removal, because git cannot delete the branch it stands on.
    Generated commands are one statement per line rather than joined with
    semicolons.
  * Say under the reflog only what can cost work: how many branches move, how
    many are removed, and how many files hold uncommitted changes. Rewind to
    this point opens the commands in a window of their own, above a branch by
    branch listing of where each one stands and where it would go.
  * Apply the Time and Entries limits to the dial as well as the table. They
    narrow the moments on offer rather than the history those moments are
    computed from, so every branch position stays accurate however far the
    limits are drawn in.
  * Copy the name of a ref from a right click in the sidebar list or on a
    label in the graph pane. The graph finds the label under the pointer
    through the lanes renderer, so a right click on the commit itself still
    opens the commit menu.
  * List HEAD above All branches in the sidebar, since HEAD is the view the
    window opens on.
  * Record one animation per scenario for the README, and give the
    repository-wide rewind a section of its own there.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Fri, 14 Aug 2026 18:30:00 +0200

gitrl-z (0.6.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Draw the preview graph as the history the planned command would leave. A
    planned branch is drawn at its target rather than at its real tip, so the
    commits the command abandons drop out of the picture. A branch that no
    plan touches is untouched, and a planned branch that no longer exists
    still contributes its target, which is what makes recreating a deleted
    branch visible.
  * Keep the commit the window opened on in the graph only while the plan is
    empty, and say so in the caption when no branch reaches it.
  * Label each step of a rebase that carried several branches with the branch
    it moved. A rebase run with --update-refs rewrites the branch it started
    on and carries every branch that points into the range. Each carried
    branch records its own move, and those records now name the steps, so the
    command offered for a step moves the branch that step belongs to. A commit
    claimed by more than one branch is left as it was.
  * Open the diff of a commit on a double click in the graph pane, as the
    context menu of the row already did.
  * Script the animation in the README and record a fuller one: a reset that
    brings back two commits, a second branch added to the same plan, a deleted
    branch offered back, then a return to the present. One script builds the
    repository, the other drives the binary in Xvfb and writes the GIF.
  * Remove the code comments from every first-party source. The copyright and
    licence headers stay, as do the comments marked for translators, and the
    vendored gitg subtree is untouched.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Thu, 13 Aug 2026 15:10:00 +0200

gitrl-z (0.5.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Offer the diff of a commit and its full hash from a right click on any row
    of the reflog list or of the graph. The diff opens in a window of its own,
    transient for the main one, and one window serves every commit.
  * Show that diff in gitg's own diff pane, vendored from gitg 44: a section
    per changed file, two columns of line numbers, the image and binary
    renderers, and the options bar at the foot. The words that changed inside
    a changed line keep their mark.
  * Add the gravatar, syntax highlighting and colour scheme rows to the
    preferences dialog, as gitg has them.
  * Open the diff window maximised, since a diff fits neither dimension of a
    fixed size.
  * Redraw the application icon without its black tile and author it as SVG.
    Each installed PNG is rendered at its own size.
  * Name the window icon at startup, so a window list or a task switcher finds
    it instead of falling back to the name of the binary.
  * Ship the AppImage's own GSettings schema. The GTK plugin of linuxdeploy
    replaced it with the build machine's copy, which aborted the AppImage at
    startup on a missing key.
  * Add a build dependency on gtksourceview-4, which gitg's diff renderer
    needs. Raise the GTK minimum from 3.20 to 3.22.

